#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Run the suite module-by-module across processes. Stdlib only, like everything else.

`python3 -m unittest discover -s tests` runs the modules one after another. On
2026-08-30 that took **589.6s** for 99 modules / 2904 tests; the same set across
8 processes took **133-285s over seven runs, median 149.7s**, on the same
machine within the same hour. That spread is the machine, not the runner — a
foreign load average of 17-65 all night — and it is quoted whole rather than
best-case on purpose. The modules are already independent — each builds its own
project under its own temp dir — so the serialisation bought nothing.

**Two guards, because the first version of this runner was wrong in the way
this repository keeps finding.** It shelled out to `python3 -m unittest
tests.<name>`, which does *not* put `tests/` on `sys.path`; `test_risks.py`
imports `PT, Project` from a sibling by bare name, failed to import, and
reported `Ran 1 test` — the loader's own error placeholder. The run came back
"1207 tests" against the serial suite's 1287 and I nearly reported it as a
speedup. **Eighty tests had stopped running and the number was still large
enough to look right.**

So: it invokes `discover` per module (which sets the path the same way the
serial run does), and it **fails on any module that contributes zero tests**.
A module that stops loading is a red, not a smaller total.

    python3 tests/parallel              # everything
    python3 tests/parallel -j 4         # fewer workers
    python3 tests/parallel test_migrate # one module, by prefix
    python3 tests/parallel --times      # + the ranked per-module wall times
    python3 tests/parallel --ids F      # + every test id and its outcome, to F
    python3 tests/parallel --record     # refresh tests/durations.json
    python3 tests/parallel --tier affected --base <ref>   # one tier (§ 5.1)

Exit status is 0 only if every module ran and every test passed.

## TASK-230: the clock, and the one rule that keeps it from lying

The suite reached 99 modules / 2904 tests and a routine full run stopped being
a nuisance and started producing wrong outcomes: on 2026-08-28 two dispatches
were killed by a 600-second no-progress watchdog **at the moment they kicked
off the suite**, and their finished work had to be recovered by hand.

Three things changed here, and the third is the only one that could ever have
altered a verdict, so it is the one with a guard on it.

**1. Longest module first.** With one worker pool the makespan is
`max(longest module, total / workers)` only if the long modules START early.
Alphabetical order does not know which those are. On the 2026-08-30 suite the
three most expensive modules — `test_task_writer`, `test_store_drift` and
`test_store_is_canonical` — sat near the end, so eight workers spent the run on
short modules and then drew the longest one with nothing left to overlap it
with.

Measured 2026-08-30 on a machine that was NOT quiet (another agent's suite runs
held the load average between 17 and 65 all night, and single measurements
swung by 2x — see `perry/evidence/2026-08/TASK-230-result.md`). Twelve full
runs, alternating the two schedules so the load drifted across both arms:

    alphabetical  (5 runs)  179.8  184.4  188.4  203.1  241.1   median 188.4s
    longest-first (7 runs)  133.1  140.1  149.2  149.7  171.9  247.0  285.0
                                                            median 149.7s

Wall-clock under that much foreign load is not a measurement, so the arms were
also compared **against each run's own per-module costs**, which removes the
drift: for each of four `--times` runs, what would the OTHER schedule have cost
on exactly these module times?

    run         alphabetical    longest-first   best possible   floor
    t-alpha-1        179.7            120.1           120.1     120.1
    t-hint-1         222.9            140.0           140.0     140.0
    t-alpha-2        241.1            155.4           154.5     154.5
    t-hint-2         210.4            133.1           133.1     133.1

**The model is validated by two of those four runs, not four.** In the two
longest-first runs the longest module started at t=0 and ran to the end, so
`simulated == max(module time) == measured wall` is an arithmetic identity and
predicts nothing. The two ALPHABETICAL runs are the real check — there the
makespan is a bin-packing of 99 modules across 8 workers that could have come
out anywhere, and it came out 179.7 against a measured 179.8, and 241.1 against
a measured 241.1. The saving is **33-37%**, and `tests/durations.json` — whose recorded
values are 3-4x too large, because they were taken under that same foreign load
— still produces a schedule equal to the perfect-knowledge one in three runs of
four and within 0.9s in the fourth. That is the hint doing the only job it has.

Total CPU is unchanged and says so: `user+sys` came to ~500s alphabetical and
~480s longest-first. The same work, finishing sooner.

`tests/durations.json` records what each module cost last time and the pool is
fed in descending order of it. Modules with no recorded time sort **first**: an
unknown module is assumed slow, because the cost of guessing wrong that way is
a slightly worse schedule and the cost of guessing wrong the other way is the
new module landing last — the very pathology above.

**2. The worker count is deliberately NOT raised, and that is a measurement.**
`min(8, cpu_count())` looks like a leftover from when there were 34 modules,
and raising it to 14 on this 14-core machine was the obvious change. It buys
**nothing**: the `floor` column above is `max(longest module, total/workers)`
and it equalled the longest module in all four runs, so once the schedule was
longest-first the binding constraint was no longer how many modules ran at once.
More workers past that point bought zero seconds and cost real contention, and
this suite has a
concurrency test (`test_host_support.TestOpenCodeDispatchLimit`) that is
already known to flake under it. **A faster-looking suite that flakes is a
worse gate than a slow one.**

The floor moved on 2026-09-01 without changing the isolation boundary:
`test_task_writer.py` was split by test class into five modules, preserving all
281 normalized `Class.test_method` ids. Its measured wall time moved from
115.5s to 27.2s, while minimal store fixtures moved the two other former
long-tail modules to 12.6s together. The worker count remains 8 until a fresh
controlled comparison shows a higher value improves the new suite rather than
only adding contention.

**3. `--ids` writes the pass/fail SET, not just its size.** The zero-test guard
catches a module that stopped loading. It does not catch a module that loaded
and ran *fewer* tests than it used to — which is the same failure wearing a
smaller hat. Diffing the id set between two runs catches both, and it is what
a change to this runner has to be verified against.

**And the set has to be able to prove it is the whole set.** The first version
of `parse_ids` returned 2885 ids for 2899 tests and said nothing; `--ids` now
refuses to write a file whose id count disagrees with unittest's own `Ran N`.
See `parse_ids`.

## TASK-251: three numbers that all looked like a failure count

A red run printed three readings a reader could take for "how many tests
failed", they disagreed, and nothing said which was which. Measured on this
repository on 2026-08-30, four failed tests across three red modules:

| reading | gave | |
|---|---|---|
| `grep -cE '^FAIL:'` over this output | **3** | wrong |
| `✗ N module(s) red` | **3** | right, about MODULES |
| sum of the per-module `FAILED (failures=N)` | **4** | the failure count |

**Only the third is the number every spec in this project is asking for**, and
it was the one no line of output stated — a reader had to sum it by hand from
the per-module verdicts, each of which sits at the bottom of its own excerpt
looking like the run's total.

The first is wrong because of the line this row is about. A red module's
stderr was echoed as `err.strip().splitlines()[-25:]` with nothing marking the
cut. `test_diagnose` fails twice; the SECOND failure's `FAIL:` header fits
inside that window and the FIRST one's does not, so the first failure reached
the log as a bare traceback with no prefix and a header count said 3 where 4
tests had failed. The TASK-249 agent found the mechanism on 2026-08-30 while
retracting a number this output had produced; it is the third agent the trap
caught in twelve hours, and one of the others had documented it in its own
result before walking into it.

The second is not wrong, it is about something else — one red module can hold
fifty failed tests — and it was the last line printed, which is where a reader
looks for a total.

So the runner now states both counts, in words that cannot be confused, and
derives neither from the excerpt:

  * `failure_block` reads the WHOLE stream and prints, above the output, how
    many of that module's tests failed and their ids. Truncation cannot reach
    a name or a number that is printed above it.
  * `failed_test_count` takes unittest's own `FAILED (failures=N, errors=M)`
    tally and the id parser's count — two origins, as `unaccounted()` already
    does for `--ids` — and reports the larger, printing the disagreement when
    there is one. A module that died before writing a verdict line has an
    UNKNOWN count and says so; None is not zero.
  * `excerpt` still windows, because a red module can emit thousands of lines,
    but it announces the elision and its size.
  * the summary is two labelled lines, `N of M MODULE(S) red` and
    `N of M TEST(S) failed`, the second being that authoritative sum.

Held by `TestARedModulesFailureCountSurvivesTheExcerpt` and
`TestTheTwoCountsAreWiredIntoMainAndNamedApart` in
`tests/test_parallel_runner.py`.

**The rule the schedule obeys: a hint may reorder the work, never select it.**
`tests/durations.json` is a **sort key over the glob's own result** and is used
nowhere else. A stale entry, a missing entry, an entry for a module that no
longer exists, or the whole file being absent or corrupt changes the ORDER the
modules run in and cannot change WHICH modules run. That is asserted directly
in `tests/test_parallel_runner.py`, because a scheduling file that can silently
drop a module is exactly the "the number was still large enough to look right"
defect this runner already has one scar from.
"""

from __future__ import annotations

import argparse
import concurrent.futures as cf
import json
import os
import pathlib
import re
import subprocess
import sys
import time

# `python3 tests/parallel` puts tests/ at sys.path[0], so this resolves. The
# runner already invokes `discover` and so cannot itself produce a load
# failure from the sibling-import cause — it imports the classifier to NAME
# the shape when a module fails to load for any of the other reasons, rather
# than printing a traceback and a `Ran 1` that the reader has to decode.
# TASK-430.
import module_run
import selection

ROOT = pathlib.Path(__file__).resolve().parent.parent
DURATIONS = ROOT / "tests" / "durations.json"
#: Bumped when the shape of `tests/durations.json` changes. 1 is TASK-304's
#: `{schema, sources, modules}`; the flat `{module: seconds}` that preceded it
#: is still read, and reports as schema 0.
SCHEMA = 1

#: `test_x (test_mod.Class.test_x)` opens a record; the outcome may land on
#: that line or, when the test has a docstring, on a following one.
_ID = re.compile(r"^(\w+) \(([\w.]+)\)")
_VERDICT = r"(ok|FAIL|ERROR|skipped .*|expected failure|unexpected success)"
_OUTCOME = re.compile(r" \.\.\. " + _VERDICT + r"$")
#: The same verdict **alone on a line**, which is where it lands whenever the
#: test itself wrote to stderr — see `parse_ids`.
_BARE_OUTCOME = re.compile(r"^" + _VERDICT + r"$")


def load_document(path: pathlib.Path | None = None) -> dict:
    """`tests/durations.json`, normalised to one shape, never raising.

    ## TASK-304: a bare number is not evidence

    Until this row the file was a flat `{module: seconds}` map and **nothing in
    it distinguished a fresh number from one that had drifted**. Measured at
    `d49964e`: 103 modules recorded against 108 on disk, two of the recorded
    ones deleted months earlier — `test_migrate.py` at 97.25, the file's
    largest entry, removed when USER-910 took migration out — and seven live
    modules unmentioned. The recorded serial total was 1968.9s against a
    measured 162.4s x 8 workers = 1299 worker-seconds, so the numbers as a set
    described no run that has ever happened.

    None of that was reported anywhere, and the argument for a stamp is
    sharper than any single wrong figure: `test_header_rule_harness.py` is
    recorded at 25.53s and measured 265.996s and 260.74s at the very commit
    that wrote the file, and `TASK-244` is about to bring that module to
    roughly 34s — at which point **25.53 starts to look plausible again and
    nothing in the file would tell you it was never measured.**

    So an entry carries a `source`, and a source carries the ref, the time,
    the worker count and the machine load its numbers were taken under. Where
    nobody recorded any of that the source says so rather than guessing; see
    the `unstamped-pre-304` note in the file itself.

    Returns `{"schema", "sources", "modules", "legacy", "unreadable"}`, where
    `modules` maps a module name to `{"sec": float | None, "source": str}`. A
    `sec` of `None` means *listed on purpose with no number* — it sorts as
    `inf` and so still runs first, which is what an unmentioned module already
    did, now by declaration instead of by omission.

    **Reading it still may not raise**, for the reason `load_durations` gives:
    a runner that refuses to start because its stopwatch is unreadable has
    turned an optimisation into an outage. Anything unparseable comes back as
    an empty document with `unreadable` set, and the caller reports it.
    """
    path = path or DURATIONS
    empty = {"schema": SCHEMA, "sources": {}, "modules": {},
             "legacy": False, "unreadable": None}
    try:
        raw = json.loads(path.read_text())
    except Exception as exc:
        return dict(empty, unreadable=f"{type(exc).__name__}: {exc}")

    if isinstance(raw, dict) and isinstance(raw.get("modules"), dict):
        mods: dict[str, dict] = {}
        for name, rec in raw["modules"].items():
            if not isinstance(name, str):
                continue
            if isinstance(rec, dict):
                sec = rec.get("sec")
                src = rec.get("source")
            else:                       # a bare number under the new shape
                sec, src = rec, None
            try:
                sec = None if sec is None else float(sec)
            except (TypeError, ValueError):
                sec = None
            mods[name] = {"sec": sec,
                          "source": src if isinstance(src, str) else None}
        srcs = raw.get("sources")
        return {"schema": raw.get("schema"),
                "sources": srcs if isinstance(srcs, dict) else {},
                "modules": mods, "legacy": False, "unreadable": None}

    # The pre-TASK-304 flat shape. Kept readable rather than rejected: this
    # file is a hint, and refusing to read an old one would cost an order for
    # nothing. Every entry in it is by definition unstamped.
    try:
        flat = {k: float(v) for k, v in raw.items() if isinstance(k, str)}
    except Exception as exc:
        return dict(empty, unreadable=f"{type(exc).__name__}: {exc}")
    return {"schema": 0, "sources": {},
            "modules": {k: {"sec": v, "source": None} for k, v in flat.items()},
            "legacy": True, "unreadable": None}


def load_durations() -> dict[str, float]:
    """Last run's per-module wall times. Any problem reading it is not an error.

    This file is a scheduling hint. It is read defensively on purpose: the
    worst a missing or malformed one may do is cost a worse ORDER, and a
    runner that refuses to run because its stopwatch is unreadable has turned
    an optimisation into an outage.

    The contract `schedule()` sees is unchanged by TASK-304: `{module:
    seconds}`, with modules the file does not give a number for simply absent,
    so they keep sorting as `inf` and running first.
    """
    doc = load_document()
    return {k: r["sec"] for k, r in doc["modules"].items()
            if r["sec"] is not None}


def git_ancestor(ref: str, root: pathlib.Path | None = None) -> bool | None:
    """Is `ref` an ancestor of the tree being scheduled? `None` = cannot tell.

    This is the whole of TASK-304's staleness test: a figure taken somewhere
    `HEAD` does not descend from describes a tree that is not this one. It is
    deliberately the *only* staleness signal — an age in days would go stale on
    a quiet weekend and a hash that is still an ancestor genuinely does
    describe an ancestor of this tree.

    Every failure mode answers `None` rather than raising: no git on the path,
    not a repository, a ref that has been garbage-collected or that lives on a
    branch this clone does not have. "I cannot tell" is a third answer and the
    report prints it as one, because reporting a ref as *stale* because git was
    missing would be the same class of silent wrongness this row exists to
    remove.
    """
    try:
        p = subprocess.run(["git", "merge-base", "--is-ancestor", ref, "HEAD"],
                           cwd=root or ROOT, capture_output=True, timeout=15)
    except Exception:
        return None
    return {0: True, 1: False}.get(p.returncode)      # 128 → None


def audit(mods: list[str], doc: dict, ancestor=git_ancestor) -> dict:
    """What the durations file says about the tree in front of it.

    `mods` is the live glob. Seven conditions, and the first three are drift the
    file may not be left in — see `drift()` — while the rest are reported
    every run and gate nothing.

    * `unlisted`  — on disk, not in the file. Seven of these at `d49964e`.
    * `phantom`   — in the file, not on disk. Two at `d49964e`, one of them
                    the largest entry in the file.
    * `dangling`  — an entry whose `source` names no block in `sources`, so
                    its provenance cannot be read even though it claims some.
    * `unmeasured`— listed on purpose with no number; runs first by design.
    * `unstamped` — has a number, and its source records no ref, so there is
                    nothing to check the number against.
    * `stale`     — has a number taken at a ref `HEAD` does not descend from.
    * `unverifiable` — a ref git could not answer for. Not stale, not current.
    """
    on_disk, recorded = set(mods), doc["modules"]
    report = {"listed": len(recorded), "on_disk": len(on_disk),
              "unlisted": sorted(on_disk - set(recorded)),
              "phantom": sorted(set(recorded) - on_disk),
              "dangling": [], "unmeasured": [], "unstamped": [],
              "stale": [], "unverifiable": [], "current": [],
              "unreadable": doc["unreadable"], "legacy": doc["legacy"]}

    verdicts: dict[str, bool | None] = {}
    for name in sorted(recorded):
        rec = recorded[name]
        src_id = rec["source"]
        src = doc["sources"].get(src_id) if isinstance(src_id, str) else None
        if src_id is not None and src is None:
            report["dangling"].append((name, src_id))
        if rec["sec"] is None:
            report["unmeasured"].append(name)
            continue
        ref = (src or {}).get("ref")
        if not isinstance(ref, str) or not ref:
            report["unstamped"].append(name)
            continue
        if ref not in verdicts:
            verdicts[ref] = ancestor(ref)
        {True: report["current"], False: report["stale"],
         None: report["unverifiable"]}[verdicts[ref]].append((name, ref))
    return report


def drift(report: dict) -> list[str]:
    """The conditions the file may not be left in, as printable lines.

    Drift only — a module that vanished and a module nobody recorded. Both
    were silent before TASK-304, and silence is what let a deleted module hold
    the head of the file's own ranking for months.

    Provenance and staleness are NOT here on purpose. Every figure the file
    inherited is unstamped, and a check that reddens the suite over that would
    have to be switched off on the day it landed; making them visible on every
    run is what the row asked for. `tests/test_durations_provenance.py` is
    what turns this list into a verdict.

    **The module name may not lead the line, and that is not cosmetic.**
    `main()` already prints `✗ {mod}` to mean "this module is RED", and the
    first version of this function produced `✗ {mod}: on disk, and …` for a
    module that had merely never been measured. `tests/test_tree_guard.py`
    caught it: that class plants a new module into a copied repo, runs the
    suite narrowed to it, and asserts `✗ {planted module}` is absent from the
    output because the plant is supposed to PASS. A brand-new module is by
    definition not in `durations.json`, so the banner claimed a passing module
    had failed. Leading with the condition instead keeps the runner's one
    marker meaning one thing.
    """
    lines = []
    for m in report["phantom"]:
        lines.append(f"no such module, still recorded: {m} — a deleted "
                     f"module's time is not a hint, it is a fossil")
    for m in report["unlisted"]:
        lines.append(f"not in durations.json: {m} — it is on disk and will "
                     f"sort as inf and run first by accident, not by decision")
    for m, src_id in report["dangling"]:
        lines.append(f"undefined source {src_id!r}: {m} — a stamp that "
                     f"cannot be read is not a stamp")
    return lines


def format_audit(report: dict) -> list[str]:
    """The banner every run prints. No flag turns it on; that is the point.

    TASK-284 was failed at V4 for adding a report nothing invoked, and the
    defect this row is fixing is a file whose wrongness nobody was told about.
    So this is called unconditionally from `main()`, and
    `tests/test_parallel_runner.py` asserts that `main()` calls it — because a
    formatter with unit tests and no caller is the same defect wearing a
    different hat.
    """
    out = [f"durations: {report['listed']} recorded · "
           f"{report['on_disk']} on disk · "
           f"{len(report['current'])} stamped at an ancestor ref · "
           f"{len(report['stale'])} stale · "
           f"{len(report['unstamped'])} unstamped · "
           f"{len(report['unmeasured'])} unmeasured"]
    if report["unreadable"]:
        out.append(f"  \033[31m! unreadable ({report['unreadable']}) — every "
                   f"module sorts as inf; the order is alphabetical\033[0m")
    if report["legacy"]:
        out.append("  ! pre-TASK-304 flat shape: no entry carries provenance")
    for line in drift(report):
        out.append(f"  \033[31m✗ {line}\033[0m")
    for name, ref in report["stale"]:
        out.append(f"  \033[33m~ {name}: measured at {ref}, which HEAD does "
                   f"not descend from\033[0m")
    if report["unverifiable"]:
        refs = sorted({r for _, r in report["unverifiable"]})
        out.append(f"  ~ {len(report['unverifiable'])} entries at refs git "
                   f"could not resolve: {', '.join(refs)}")
    if not drift(report) and not report["stale"] and not report["unreadable"]:
        out.append("  ✓ every module on disk is accounted for")
    return out


def schedule(mods: list[str], durations: dict[str, float]) -> list[str]:
    """Longest first; unknown modules first of all.

    `mods` is returned permuted and otherwise untouched — same length, same
    membership. Nothing in here may filter, and `tests/test_parallel_runner.py`
    holds that line.
    """
    return sorted(mods, key=lambda m: (-durations.get(m, float("inf")), m))


def parse_ids(stderr: str) -> list[tuple[str, str]]:
    """(test id, outcome) pairs out of `unittest -v` output.

    **The verdict is not always on the line that names the test, and the first
    version of this function silently dropped every case where it was not.**
    `unittest` writes the description and ` ... ` when the test STARTS and the
    verdict when it finishes, so anything the test writes to stderr in between
    lands in the middle — and the verdict is then pushed onto a line of its
    own. Measured on the live suite: 2899 tests ran and this function returned
    2885 ids, fourteen missing across seven modules, the shortfall coming from
    ordinary `DeprecationWarning`s that `-W default` prints.

    That is the exact defect `tests/parallel` already carries a scar from —
    **the number was still large enough to look right** — reappearing in the
    function whose whole job is to say which tests ran. `--ids` is the pass/
    fail SET, so a set that quietly understates makes every comparison drawn
    from it a false negative: a test that stopped running looks like a test the
    parser never saw.

    Two shapes are read, and `main()` refuses to write an `--ids` file when the
    count does not match unittest's own `Ran N` — because a third shape is
    always possible (a test whose output does not end in a newline glues the
    verdict onto it) and a parser that cannot account for every test must say
    so rather than round down.
    """
    out: list[tuple[str, str]] = []
    pending: str | None = None
    for line in stderr.splitlines():
        m = _ID.match(line)
        if m:
            pending = m.group(2)
        if not pending:
            continue
        o = _OUTCOME.search(line) or (not m and _BARE_OUTCOME.match(line))
        if o:
            out.append((pending, o.group(1).split()[0]))
            pending = None
    return out


def unaccounted(results: list[dict]) -> list[dict]:
    """Modules whose id list does not account for every test unittest ran.

    Two numbers with independent origins: `ran` is summed from unittest's own
    `Ran N` line, the ids come from parsing the verbose stream. They must
    agree, and when they do not the SET is the thing that is wrong — not the
    count — so the caller refuses to write it rather than writing a set that
    silently understates. See `parse_ids`.
    """
    return [r for r in results if len(r["ids"]) != r["ran"]]


#: unittest's own trailing verdict, e.g. `FAILED (failures=2, errors=1)`.
_FAILED_SUMMARY = re.compile(r"^FAILED \((?P<body>.*)\)$", re.MULTILINE)
_BAD_COUNT = re.compile(r"\b(?:failures|errors)=(\d+)")

#: How many lines of a red module's own output are echoed. The number is not
#: the point; SAYING that it is a window is (TASK-251).
TAIL_LINES = 25


def failing_ids(r: dict) -> list[tuple[str, str]]:
    """The (test id, outcome) pairs this module reported FAIL or ERROR.

    Read from the whole verbose stream, never from the excerpt — which is the
    entire point of this function existing. See `failure_block`.
    """
    return [(i, o) for i, o in r["ids"] if o in ("FAIL", "ERROR")]


def unittest_bad_count(stderr: str) -> int | None:
    """failures+errors as **unittest itself** totalled them, or None if it
    never got as far as saying.

    A second origin for a number `failing_ids` also produces, on the same
    principle `unaccounted()` already runs on: the id parser reads the verbose
    stream test by test, this reads the one line unittest writes at the end.
    They have to agree, and when they do not the runner says so instead of
    picking one silently.

    None is not zero. A module killed mid-run, or one whose import exploded
    before the runner was built, writes no verdict line at all — and reporting
    that as "0 tests failed" under a red module is the kind of number this
    row exists to remove.
    """
    n = None
    for m in _FAILED_SUMMARY.finditer(stderr):
        n = (n or 0) + sum(int(v) for v in _BAD_COUNT.findall(m.group("body")))
    return n


def failed_test_count(r: dict) -> int:
    """How many TESTS this module failed — the count the summary line sums.

    unittest's own tally and the id parser's are combined with `max`, not
    averaged and not silently preferred: the failure this project keeps
    finding is a number that UNDERSTATES and still looks right, so when the
    two disagree the larger is the one that cannot hide a failure. The
    disagreement itself is printed by `failure_block`.
    """
    claimed = unittest_bad_count(r["err"])
    named = len(failing_ids(r))
    return named if claimed is None else max(claimed, named)


def excerpt(err: str, limit: int = TAIL_LINES) -> str:
    """A module's output, tail-windowed — and **the window says so**.

    This printed `splitlines()[-25:]` with nothing marking the cut. On
    2026-08-30 the TASK-249 agent was retracting a failure count it had got
    from this output when it found why: when a module fails twice, the second
    failure's `FAIL:` header fits inside the window and the first one's does
    not, so a reader counting headers counts one failure where there were two.
    Every spec in this project asks for a baseline failure count from this
    runner, so a silent elision here corrupts the evidence every row rests on.

    Truncation stays — a red module can emit thousands of lines — but it is
    now announced, with the count of what was dropped and the total it was
    dropped from, so the reader knows the excerpt is an excerpt. The
    authoritative counts are printed ABOVE it by `failure_block`, where no
    window can reach them.
    """
    lines = err.strip().splitlines()
    if len(lines) <= limit:
        return "\n".join(lines)
    dropped = len(lines) - limit
    return "\n".join(
        [f"  \033[33m… {dropped} earlier line(s) elided of {len(lines)}; "
         f"the last {limit} follow — re-run this module alone for all of "
         f"it\033[0m"] + lines[-limit:])


def failure_block(r: dict) -> str:
    """Everything printed about one red module. Counts first, excerpt last.

    The order is the fix. `failing_ids` and `unittest_bad_count` both read the
    WHOLE stream, so the names and the number of failed tests are established
    before any windowing happens and are printed above it. The excerpt below
    them is then free to be a window, because nothing a reader needs to count
    lives only inside it.
    """
    bad = failing_ids(r)
    claimed = unittest_bad_count(r["err"])
    out = []
    # **Before any count is printed, say whether the module ran at all.**
    # TASK-430. A module that failed to import reports `Ran 1 test` — the
    # loader's `_FailedTest` placeholder — and every line below this one would
    # then describe that placeholder as though it were the module's test set.
    # The counts stay (they are true of what unittest did); what this adds is
    # the sentence saying they are not true of the module.
    load_verdict = module_run.load_failure_verdict(r["mod"], r["err"])
    if load_verdict is not None:
        out.append(f"\n\033[31m✗ {load_verdict}\033[0m")
    if claimed is None:
        out.append(f"\n\033[31m✗ {r['mod']}\033[0m — exited {r['rc']} "
                   f"without a unittest verdict line, so how many tests failed "
                   f"is NOT known from this run; the {len(bad)} named below "
                   f"are only the ones the stream got as far as reporting.")
    else:
        out.append(f"\n\033[31m✗ {r['mod']}\033[0m — "
                   f"{failed_test_count(r)} of {r['ran']} test(s) failed")
        if claimed != len(bad):
            out.append(f"  \033[31m! unittest counted {claimed} failed test(s) "
                       f"and the id parser named {len(bad)}\033[0m — the "
                       f"list below is the parser's and is short; the count "
                       f"above is the larger of the two on purpose.")
    for i, o in bad:
        out.append(f"    {o:<5}  {i}")
    out.append(excerpt(r["err"]))
    return "\n".join(out)
def head_ref(root: pathlib.Path | None = None) -> str | None:
    """The commit a measurement was taken at, or `None` if that is unknowable.

    `None` is written into the file as a null ref rather than as a plausible
    string, and the audit then reports the entries as unstamped. A measurement
    whose ref is a guess is worse than one that admits it has none.
    """
    try:
        p = subprocess.run(["git", "rev-parse", "HEAD"], cwd=root or ROOT,
                           capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=15)
    except Exception:
        return None
    return p.stdout.strip() or None if p.returncode == 0 else None


def write_record(results: list[dict], workers: int,
                 path: pathlib.Path | None = None) -> str:
    """Write `tests/durations.json` with this run stamped onto every figure.

    The stamp is the row's deliverable. It carries:

    * **ref** — what tree was measured, so `git_ancestor` can later say
      whether it is still this one.
    * **taken** — when, in UTC, so two sources can be ordered.
    * **workers** — the pool these times came out of. A module's wall time is
      not independent of how many others were competing with it.
    * **load1** — the 1-minute load average at the END of the run. The 2026-08
      measurements in this module's docstring swung 2x on foreign load alone,
      and TASK-230 quoted its spread whole rather than best-case for exactly
      that reason. A number recorded at load 30 is a different kind of claim
      from one recorded on a quiet machine, and the file should say which.

    Sources accumulate: earlier ones are kept, so a file can honestly hold
    figures from more than one measurement without pretending they came from
    one run.
    """
    path = path or DURATIONS
    prior = load_document(path)
    stamp = time.strftime("%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ", time.gmtime())
    src_id = f"{stamp[:10]}-{stamp[11:19].replace(':', '')}"
    try:
        load1 = round(os.getloadavg()[0], 2)
    except (OSError, AttributeError):
        load1 = None
    sources = dict(prior["sources"])
    sources[src_id] = {"ref": head_ref(), "taken": stamp, "workers": workers,
                       "load1": load1,
                       "note": f"`tests/parallel --record -j {workers}`."}
    modules = {r["mod"]: {"sec": round(r["sec"], 2), "source": src_id}
               for r in sorted(results, key=lambda r: r["mod"])}
    # A source no entry points at is not history, it is litter — and a
    # `sources` block that grows every run makes the ones that matter harder
    # to see. Pruned rather than kept.
    used = {rec["source"] for rec in modules.values()}
    doc = {"schema": SCHEMA,
           "sources": {k: v for k, v in sources.items() if k in used},
           "modules": modules}
    path.write_text(json.dumps(doc, indent=1, sort_keys=False) + "\n")
    return src_id


def run_module(name: str) -> dict:
    t = time.time()
    proc = subprocess.run(
        [sys.executable, "-m", "unittest", "discover", "-s", "tests",
         "-p", name, "-v"],
        capture_output=True, text=True, cwd=ROOT)
    ran = sum(int(line.split()[1])
              for line in proc.stderr.splitlines() if line.startswith("Ran "))
    return {"mod": name, "rc": proc.returncode, "ran": ran,
            "sec": time.time() - t, "err": proc.stderr,
            "ids": parse_ids(proc.stderr)}


#: **The harness's own self-tests, left out of a default run.** TASK-400.
#:
#: These three assert things about `tests/` rather than about Perry: that the
#: tree guard can fail, that the scheduler may reorder but never drop, and that
#: `durations.json` describes the tree in front of it. They cost 52.4 of the
#: suite's seconds and `test_tree_guard` alone is 41.4 of that, at 1.7s per
#: test, because each case unpacks a repository copy.
#:
#: **The guard itself does NOT move.** `tests/run` step 0 runs
#: `tests/tree_guard.py` on every exit path including `--lint`, and that is a
#: different file from `tests/test_tree_guard.py`. What is deferred is the
#: proof that the guard can go red, not the guard.
#:
#: `test_restore_check` was considered and kept: it tests
#: `bin/perry-restore-check`, which is a product tool a mutation round depends
#: on, not part of this harness.
#:
#: Run them with `bash tests/run --slow` or `python3 tests/parallel --slow`.
HARNESS_SELF_TESTS = frozenset({
    "test_tree_guard.py",
    "test_parallel_runner.py",
    "test_durations_provenance.py",
})


def main() -> int:
    ap = argparse.ArgumentParser(description=__doc__.splitlines()[0])
    ap.add_argument("only", nargs="*", help="module name prefixes to run")
    ap.add_argument("-j", type=int, default=min(8, (os.cpu_count() or 4)),
                    help="worker processes (default: min(8, cpus); see the "
                         "module docstring for why this is not cpu_count)")
    ap.add_argument("--times", action="store_true",
                    help="print the ranked per-module wall times")
    ap.add_argument("--ids", metavar="FILE",
                    help="write every test id and its outcome to FILE")
    ap.add_argument("--record", action="store_true",
                    help="refresh tests/durations.json from this run")
    ap.add_argument("--slow", action="store_true",
                    help="also run the harness's own self-tests, which the "
                         "default run leaves out (see HARNESS_SELF_TESTS)")
    ap.add_argument("--tier", choices=selection.TIERS,
                    help="run one of DESIGN-021 § 5.1's four tiers; the module "
                         "set is tests/selection.py's, not a second copy of it")
    ap.add_argument("--base", metavar="REF",
                    help="with --tier affected: the ref the change is "
                         "diffed against")
    ap.add_argument("--alphabetical", action="store_true",
                    help="ignore the stopwatch and run modules in name order "
                         "— the pre-TASK-230 schedule, kept so the saving can "
                         "be re-measured rather than believed")
    args = ap.parse_args()

    all_mods = sorted(p.name for p in (ROOT / "tests").glob("test_*.py"))

    # ── the tiers (DESIGN-021 § 5.1, TASK-449) ──────────────────────────
    # **The module set comes from `tests/selection.py` and is not recomputed
    # here.** `tests/run --tier` shells out to this runner, so if the two
    # disagreed about what a tier contains, the selection `tests/run` printed
    # and the modules this process ran would be different sets — with nothing
    # on screen saying so. That is the same shape as the defect in this file's
    # own history: a number large enough to look right.
    #
    # `--tier` refuses to combine with the three flags that also decide the
    # set, rather than picking a winner. `--record` runs the whole tree always
    # (above), `--slow` is the spelling of `--tier slow`, and a positional
    # prefix is a narrowing the caller typed by hand; silently letting one
    # override a tier is the accepted-and-dropped defect DESIGN-016 goal 12
    # names. `--base` without `--tier` is refused for the same reason.
    tier_plan = None
    if args.base and not args.tier:
        print("--base is only read with --tier affected", file=sys.stderr)
        return 2
    if args.tier:
        clash = [f for f, on in (("--slow", args.slow), ("--record", args.record),
                                 ("a module prefix", bool(args.only))) if on]
        if clash:
            print(f"--tier {args.tier} decides which modules run; "
                  f"{' and '.join(clash)} decides it too — pass one",
                  file=sys.stderr)
            return 2
        if args.tier == selection.SMOKE:
            # Accepting it would print "0 modules · 0 tests · ✓ all green",
            # which is a green from a run that verified nothing — the one
            # outcome this runner may not produce (NN-3).
            print("the smoke tier runs no test module: its checks are "
                  "`tests/run`'s steps 1 and 3 and the tree guard's hash "
                  "check.\n  run `bash tests/run --tier smoke`", file=sys.stderr)
            return 2
        try:
            tier_plan = selection.plan(args.tier, args.base, root=ROOT,
                                       slow=HARNESS_SELF_TESTS)
        except (selection.TierError, selection.GitError,
                selection.DeclarationError) as e:
            print(f"tests/parallel --tier {args.tier}: {e}", file=sys.stderr)
            return 2
        # Goal 6: every run prints what it selected and why, before it runs.
        for line in selection.plan_lines(tier_plan, base=args.base, dry=False,
                                         durations=load_durations(), root=ROOT):
            print(line)
        if not tier_plan.modules:
            print(f"no test module is selected for --tier {args.tier}"
                  + (f" --base {args.base}" if args.base else ""),
                  file=sys.stderr)
            return 2
    # **The self-test filter applies to the DEFAULT set only.** Naming a module
    # explicitly reaches it whether or not a plain run would have picked it up:
    # `--only test_tree_guard` means run that, and answering "no test module
    # matches" to a name that is sitting on disk is a lie. Filtering before
    # `--only` did exactly that, and `test_parallel_runner`'s own cases caught
    # it — they drive `main()` with `mod: "test_parallel_runner.py"`, which is
    # in the set, and got exit 2 instead of 0.
    if tier_plan is not None:
        mods = list(tier_plan.modules)
    elif args.only:
        mods = [m for m in all_mods
                if any(m.startswith(p.removesuffix(".py")) for p in args.only)]
        if not mods:
            print(f"no test module matches {args.only}", file=sys.stderr)
            return 2
    elif args.slow or args.record:
        # **`--record` runs the whole tree, always.** `durations.json` is a
        # statement about every module on disk — `test_durations_provenance`
        # reads it that way and reddens when it is not — so recording from a
        # narrowed run writes a file that is wrong about the tree rather than
        # merely stale. Before this line, `python3 tests/parallel --record`
        # (line 30 of this file's own usage) rewrote 121 entries as 118 and
        # deleted the three in `HARNESS_SELF_TESTS`, one of which is the module
        # that gates the file. So a default run afterwards printed "all green"
        # over a file this repository's own check refuses.
        #
        # Implying the full set rather than refusing: the documented
        # invocation keeps working and cannot produce a partial file, which is
        # the outcome either spelling has to reach. `--only` still SKIPS
        # recording a few lines below, because a caller who named one module
        # did not ask to re-time the tree.
        mods = all_mods
    else:
        mods = [m for m in all_mods if m not in HARNESS_SELF_TESTS]

    t0 = time.time()
    with cf.ThreadPoolExecutor(max_workers=args.j) as ex:
        order = schedule(mods, {} if args.alphabetical else load_durations())
        results = list(ex.map(run_module, order))
    results.sort(key=lambda r: r["mod"])
    wall = time.time() - t0

    failed = [r for r in results if r["rc"] != 0]
    empty = [r for r in results if r["rc"] == 0 and r["ran"] == 0]
    total = sum(r["ran"] for r in results)

    for r in failed:
        print(failure_block(r))
    for r in empty:
        print(f"\n\033[31m✗ {r['mod']} ran zero tests\033[0m — it loaded "
              f"without error and contributed nothing. A module that stops "
              f"running is a failure, not a smaller total.")

    if args.times:
        # The recorded column is the one that makes a drifted figure visible
        # at a glance. `test_header_rule_harness.py` sat at 25.53 recorded
        # against 260-266 measured for weeks with nothing printing the two
        # side by side (TASK-304).
        recorded = load_durations()
        print("\n  seconds  recorded    x  tests  module")
        for r in sorted(results, key=lambda r: -r["sec"]):
            was = recorded.get(r["mod"])
            ratio = (f"{r['sec'] / was:4.1f}"
                     if was else ("   -" if was is None else "   ∞"))
            print(f"  {r['sec']:7.2f}  {'      -' if was is None else f'{was:7.2f}'}"
                  f"  {ratio}  {r['ran']:5d}  {r['mod']}")

    short = unaccounted(results)
    if args.ids:
        # **A set that cannot account for every test is not the set.** `ran`
        # comes from unittest's own `Ran N` line and the ids come from the
        # verbose stream: two numbers with independent origins that must agree.
        # Checked only under `--ids`, because that is the mode whose whole
        # output is the set — a run that is only asked for a verdict is not
        # made red by a line the parser could not read.
        for r in short:
            print(f"\n\033[31m✗ {r['mod']}: unittest ran {r['ran']} tests and "
                  f"the id parser accounted for {len(r['ids'])}\033[0m — "
                  f"`--ids` would understate the set, which is the one thing "
                  f"it may not do.")
        if short:
            print("\033[31m✗ no --ids file written\033[0m")
        else:
            pathlib.Path(args.ids).write_text("".join(
                f"{i}\t{o}\n" for r in results for i, o in sorted(r["ids"])))

    if args.record and not args.only:
        # **Still only under `--record`.** TASK-304 deliberately did not make
        # this automatic: a run that re-measured on every invocation would
        # replace a stale number with a load-contaminated one, and this
        # machine has had a foreign load average above 20 for whole evenings.
        # What the row added is that the numbers now say what they were taken
        # under, so a reader can decide whether to believe them.
        src_id = write_record(results, args.j)
        # `relative_to` RAISES on a path outside the tree, and a line that only
        # says where the file went must not be able to end the run. A test that
        # redirects `DURATIONS` to a temp file — which is how a `--record` case
        # avoids rewriting the live stopwatch — hit exactly that.
        try:
            where = DURATIONS.relative_to(ROOT)
        except ValueError:
            where = DURATIONS
        print(f"recorded {len(results)} module times to {where} "
              f"under source {src_id!r}")

    print(f"\n{len(results)} modules · {total} tests · {wall:.1f}s "
          f"· {args.j} workers")
    # Unconditional, and after the verdict so it is the last thing on screen.
    # No flag turns this on: the defect TASK-304 fixed was a file nobody was
    # told was wrong, and a report you have to ask for would have left it that
    # way. It does not change the exit status — `tests/parallel` may not
    # become an outage over its own stopwatch — and
    # `tests/test_durations_provenance.py` is what turns drift into a red.
    print()
    for line in format_audit(audit(all_mods, load_document())):
        print(line)
    if failed or empty:
        # **Two counts, named, on two lines — because one number that could be
        # read as either is how TASK-249 mis-reported a baseline.** The module
        # count is what the exit status is about; the test count is what every
        # spec asking for "the baseline failure count" wants. Neither is
        # derived from the printed excerpts, so truncation cannot move them.
        bad_tests = sum(failed_test_count(r) for r in failed)
        unknown = [r for r in failed if unittest_bad_count(r["err"]) is None]
        print(f"\033[31m✗ {len(failed) + len(empty)} of {len(results)} "
              f"MODULE(S) red\033[0m"
              + (f" — {len(empty)} of them ran zero tests" if empty else ""))
        print(f"\033[31m✗ {bad_tests} of {total} TEST(S) failed\033[0m"
              + (f" — plus {len(unknown)} module(s) that exited without a "
                 f"unittest verdict line, whose failed-test count is unknown "
                 f"and is NOT in that total" if unknown else ""))
        return 1
    if args.ids and short:
        return 1
    if tier_plan is not None and args.tier == selection.AFFECTED:
        # **`affected` may not print "all green".** The sentence a reviewer
        # reads off a result is the last green line in it, and this run was 29
        # of 146 modules. `full` and `slow` keep the plain banner: one is the
        # suite minus the slow tier — what a bare run has always meant here —
        # and the other is the whole tree. `smoke` never reaches this line.
        print(f"\033[32m✓ green for --tier {args.tier} — this is NOT a green "
              f"suite\033[0m")
        return 0
    print("\033[32m✓ all green\033[0m")
    return 0


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main())
